          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_1 \to R
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space"} space
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_1
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_\bullet
            (with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
            in some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebra"} convolution algebra
            structure from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            operation on morphisms. This is called its
            %em category convolution algebra
            or just
            %em category algebra
            for short.
          %p
            Often this is considered specifically for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            %em groupoid convolution algebra
            or just
            %em groupoid algebra
            for short. (For one-object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
            groupoids of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groups"} groups
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebras"} group algebras
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse"} inversion operation
            of the groupoid naturally makes its groupoid algebra into a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-algebra"} star-algebra
            (this is generally the case for category algebras of
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+categories"} dagger categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} C-star-algebra
            theory.
          %p
            More generally, if the groupoid carries a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+2-bundle"} line 2-bundle
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbe"} bundle gerbe
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+bundle"} line bundle
            (satisfying some compatibility conditions), and one can consider convolution algebras not just of functions, but of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sections"} sections
            of this line bundle. The resulting algebra is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em twisted groupoid convolution algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+class"} characteristic class
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+2-bundle"} line 2-bundle
            (
            %a{:href => "#TXLG"}> TXLG
            ).
          %p
            For “bare” categories/groupoids (i.e.:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} internal
            to
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stuff%2C+structure%2C+property"} structure
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+categories"} internal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+groupoids"} internal groupoids
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometric
            contexts, notably in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+groupoids"}> topological groupoids
            ),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groupoids"}> Lie groupoids
            ), and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coalgebra"} coalgebra
            structure over the functions on its canonical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            is also called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebroid"} Hopf algebroid
            and may be used to
            %em characterize
            the geometric groupoid.
          %p
            Therefore, to some extent, one may think of the relation between groupoids/categories and their groupoid/category algebras as an incarnation of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isbell+duality"} general duality
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry"} geometry
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Connes094"} Connes 94
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"} star product
            and denoted “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            ”. Groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            form a rich sub-class of all
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C%2A-algebras"} C*-algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+product+C%2A-algebras"} crossed product C*-algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cuntz+algebras"} Cuntz algebras
          %p
            Groupoid convolution algebras may also be understood as generalizations of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+algebras"} matrix algebras
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pair+groupoid"} pair groupoid
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Connes094"} Connes 94, 1.1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Werner+Heisenberg"} Werner Heisenberg
            (re-)discovered (infinite-dimensional)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+algebras"} matrix algebras
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebras+of+observables"} algebras of observables
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#EH"} EH 06
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            is given fairly generally by twisted groupoid convolution algebras. See
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization+of+symplectic+groupoids"} geometric quantization of symplectic groupoids
            for more on this. For the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+groupoid"} discrete
            but
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Dijkgraaf-Witten+theory"} infinity-Dijkgraaf-Witten theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FHLT"} FHLT 09
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#for_bare_categories_discrete_geometry For bare categories (discrete geometry)
          #CategoryAlgebra.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            %p
              The
              %strong category algebra
              or
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convolution+algebra"} convolution algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[\mathcal{C}]
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              is the
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} algebra
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  whose underlying
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
                  is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+module"} free module
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R[\mathcal{C}_1]
                  over the set of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                  of
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝒞
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %li
                %p
                  whose product operation is defined on
                  = succeed "-elements" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/basis"} basis
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ,
                        %mi g
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi 𝒞
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ↪
                        %mi R
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi 𝒞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f,g \in \mathcal{C}_1 \hookrightarrow R[\mathcal{C}]
                  to be their
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                  if they are composable and zero otherwise:
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mi g
